Based on Passage [2], if your loan is for **the current or upcoming school year**, you should contact your **school's financial aid office** directly for information about loan status. If this information needs updating (e.g., due to changes in school enrollment, withdrawal, or other major events), keep the contact details up to date with the school and then contact a student loan servicer when you:
- change your name, address, or phone number;
- need help making your payment;
- have questions about your bill; or
- have other questions about your student loan.

If your loan was disbursed in a **past school year** and you are still in school, contact the loan servicer when you:
- withdraw from school;
- graduate (after graduating) to continue earning while in school;
- drop below half-time enrollment until higher education is available to you; or stop going to school.

The information about who your bank account manager is and whether your loan is owned by the federal government is not included in [2].

Based on passage **[1]**, if your student loan was disbursed in a past school year, you are still in school. According to the text under "Contact Information for Loans Not Owned by ED," in this situation you should keep contact information up to date with **your own school** and then contact your loan servicer when:
- You withdraw;
- Graduate;
- Drop below half-time enrollment; or
- Stop going to school.

Based on the text in **[1]**, if you are currently delinquent on payments by 90 days or more, your loan servicer will report this status to three major national credit bureaus. Even if you continue making payments but remain delinquent beyond 60 days [2], the risk is that a default could eventually occur. If you cannot pay and do not file for repaid early [1] or get financial aid waived under Section 8 of the Student Loan Forgiveness Act, your loan can go into default, which leads to serious consequences such as:

*   Damage to your credit rating;
*   Future inability to borrow (e.g., on credit cards, home/car loans);
*   Garnishment of wages; and
*   Withholding of tax refunds.

The text notes that while it is a debt you are obligated to repay, some situations allow for forgiven or discharged amounts [3]. However, regarding **[1]**'s direct instruction on what happens with the specific 90-day delinquency period: once reported, you must contact your loan servicer. Even if payments continue, continuing delinquency beyond 60 days (at least as of July 6, 2024) leads to a default risk [1]. Since there is no general legal provision stating that a $5,000 student loan becomes non-exhaustive upon going into default in the context of federal law
